<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head></head><body style="color: rgb(32, 33, 34); font-family: verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px;"><p style="text-align: center; margin: 0in; font-size: 12pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif;"><b><span style="font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;">Case Study: Social Work Values, Ethics, and Multicultural Competence </span></b></p>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 0in; font-size: 12pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif;">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-indent: 0.5in; line-height: 200%; margin: 0in; font-size: 12pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif;"><span style="font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;">Your supervisor approaches you to begin work with a new client. This client is a 24-year-old Deaf woman and a single mom who recently was reported to child protective. The initial report to Child Protective comes from a neighbor in the apartment building where the client lives. The report is that the client’s 3-year-old daughter has been roaming around in the entryway and twice outside. During each interaction, the neighbor described the child wearing dirty clothing and a full diaper.</span></p>
<p style="text-indent: 0.5in; line-height: 200%; margin: 0in; font-size: 12pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif;"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;">To meet with the client, you call the number on file, which sends you to an automated system with interpreter 5423. You instantly hang up, thinking someone wrote down the wrong number. You see a phone number for texting in the file. You text the client, “Hi, I am your new case manager, and I want to set up a time to discuss your needs.” There is no response for 24 hours. You send another text the next day, “We need to meet soon because Child Protective is trying to set up a meeting. Can you meet tomorrow?” That night, at 8:30 p.m., the client responded, “I not know you no ASL my right ADA… love my daughter no help you hate Deaf people no understand.”</span></p></body></html>